Understanding the Recursive Flow

Consider this part of the code:

if (i >= m || j >= n) {
    return;
}

if (i == m - 1 && j == n - 1) {
    cnt++;
    return;
}

solve(i, j + 1);
solve(i + 1, j);
1. Boundary Condition
if (i >= m || j >= n) {
    return;
}

This condition checks whether the current position (i, j) has gone outside the grid.

If the condition is true, return stops the current recursive call immediately.

If the condition is false, nothing happens inside the if block and the program continues to the next statement.

Since solve() is a void function, return; does not return any value. It simply exits the current function call.

2. Destination Condition
if (i == m - 1 && j == n - 1) {
    cnt++;
    return;
}

This condition checks whether the robot has reached the bottom-right cell.

If the current position is the destination:

cnt++;

increases the number of valid paths by 1.

Then:

return;

stops exploring further from that position.

3. Moving Right
solve(i, j + 1);

If neither of the previous conditions is true, the robot first tries to move right.

Here:

i remains the same
j increases by 1

For example:

(0,0)
  ↓
(0,1)
  ↓
(0,2)
  ↓
(0,3)

The recursive calls keep increasing j along this branch until one of two things happens:

Destination is reached
        OR
j becomes greater than or equal to n

If j >= n, this condition becomes true:

if (i >= m || j >= n)

and the current call returns.

4. Backtracking and Moving Down

After:

solve(i, j + 1);

finishes, the program comes back to the previous function call and executes:

solve(i + 1, j);

Now the robot moves down.

Here:

i increases by 1
j remains the same

So the recursion explores both possibilities:

              (i,j)
              /   \
           Right   Down
         (i,j+1) (i+1,j)

This process continues until every possible valid path from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner has been explored.

Example

For a 2 × 3 grid:

(0,0)   (0,1)   (0,2)
(1,0)   (1,1)   (1,2)
                   ↑
                Destination

Starting from (0,0), the recursion first explores the right side:

(0,0) → (0,1) → (0,2) → (0,3)

At (0,3):

j >= n
3 >= 3

is true, so the function returns.

It then backtracks to (0,2) and tries moving down:

(0,2) → (1,2)

Now (1,2) is the destination, so:

cnt++;

is executed.

The same process of moving, returning, and exploring the other branch continues until all possible paths are checked.
